{# Plain-text limitations (single source). One paragraph per analysis: what it cannot conclude. #}
Clinical features. The phenotype used here is the HPO set released to hackathon participants, which is not the complete clinical record. Under the feature-absence rule stated in the methods, no negative phenotype claim is made anywhere in this report, and conclusions that would depend on a feature being genuinely absent are stated conditionally. A reader with access to the full record may be able to resolve those conditionals; this analysis cannot.

Variant phase. The two BUB1B variants are called heterozygous and treated throughout as compound heterozygous, one on each allele. That configuration is inferred, not demonstrated. No parental genotypes were released, and this data was checked and cannot phase the pair: the variants lie about 11 kb apart, the interval between them holds a single heterozygous site in both this pipeline's calls and the distributed VCF, and the gaps on either side of it exceed what any short-read fragment spans, so read-backed phasing cannot connect them; population phasing carries no information at these allele frequencies. Four things support the inference. MVA1 is recessive, so a cis configuration would make the child an unaffected carrier, as the monoallelic parents in the original families were {{ cite('[@hanks2004]') }}. That recessive requirement is not only inferred here: Gene2Phenotype curates this gene-disease pair (record G2P00148) as biallelic autosomal with a loss-of-function mechanism and a variant consequence of absent gene product, at its highest confidence level, definitive, from five publications {{ cite('[@thormann2019]') }}. A cis configuration would also require a second, undetected hit on the other allele, and the search for one in this data — structural variants, exon copy number and rare functional panel variants in BUB1B — found nothing, though a deep-intronic or regulatory hit would be invisible to it. And one truncating allele with one missense allele is the canonical MVA1 genotype {{ cite('[@suijkerbuijk2010]') }}. Genotyping the parents at the two positions would settle it, and would also show whether both variants were inherited, which the pregnancy-loss argument in the mechanism section additionally assumes.

Missense allele. No formal variant classification (ACMG) was performed. The missense allele has no ClinVar record, is ultra-rare in gnomAD and is predicted damaging by AlphaMissense (the modifier-panel table gives the values), but nothing in this analysis measures the protein's residual activity. That the allele is a hypomorph rather than a null is an inference from the diagnosis and the canonical MVA1 genotype {{ cite('[@suijkerbuijk2010]') }}, and every statement that acts on residual protein — the proteostasis axis and the conditional residual-function inference — inherits it.

Pharmacogenomics, panel coverage. The pharmacogene panel is the 20 genes listed in the methods plus CFTR, HLA-A, HLA-B and MT-RNR1. Genes bearing on this child's chemotherapy that lie outside that panel are named in the report's panel-gap table. They were scoped out of the analysis, not absent from the data: the sequencing is whole-genome and covers them at full depth, and the same force-calling approach used for the PharmCAT positions could genotype them. Stage 1b closes part of this gap automatically, because any position carrying a ClinPGx annotation at an asserted evidence level, for a drug on the candidate list, is force-called from the CRAM and joined regardless of panel membership. What remains genuinely uncovered is therefore narrower than the gene list suggests: genes with no ClinPGx annotation at the asserted levels, and any agent the candidate list omits.

Pharmacogenomics, ancestry and definition coverage. Star-allele definition tables are better populated for European haplotypes, so the reassurance carried by a reference call is ancestry-dependent, and this report has no machinery to express that: a *1/*1 is reported identically whatever the sample's ancestry. Alleles enriched outside European populations and variably covered include DPYD c.557A>G (rs115232898), CYP2C9 *5, *6, *8 and *11, CYP2D6 *17 and *29, and much of the NAT2 haplotype distribution. {% if qc and qc.ancestry %}For this sample the genome QC branch predicts {{ qc.ancestry.predicted }} ({% for k, v in qc.ancestry.probabilities.items() if v > 0 %}{{ k }} {{ v }}{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}), a coarse superpopulation assignment rather than a haplotype-level statement. {% endif %}Run on a genome of different ancestry, this report would make equally confident reference calls with no qualifier attached.

Pharmacogenomics, what caller agreement is worth. Three callers agreeing is reproducibility, not validation. PharmCAT, Aldy and PyPGx read the same reads from the same alignment against the same reference, and their definitions share provenance (PharmVar / ClinPGx), so concordance controls for implementation bugs in any one caller but cannot detect reference bias, mismapping, or a gap shared by the definition tables. The single discordance in this run, RYR1, was itself definition coverage rather than a calling difference. Nothing in this project has been run against a sample with consensus genotypes, so every accuracy statement rests on internal consistency; a GeT-RM reference sample through the identical pipeline is the missing control.

Pharmacogenomics. Calls come from short-read WGS with research tools; clinical pharmacogenetic testing uses validated assays. Allele-definition versions differ between callers (PharmCAT, Aldy, PyPGx), so a disagreement can reflect definition coverage rather than genotype. Two genes (VKORC1, IFNL3) cannot be compared between callers without assumptions. HLA typing is 2-field from short reads; class II is single-caller. Rare or novel alleles absent from the reference definitions are reported as undocumented variation, not interpreted. MT-RNR1 was measured in the sequenced sample; heteroplasmy can differ between tissues — including the cochlea, the tissue where aminoglycoside risk is decided — and reads from nuclear mitochondrial segments (NUMTs) can add low-level apparent heteroplasmy, which is why allele fractions below the heteroplasmy threshold are reported but not called. The clinical contexts marked 'syndrome' (antiepileptic therapy, anaesthesia, supportive care) are inferred from the published MVA1 series, not from this child's record. Open Targets sources its pharmacogenomics from ClinPGx (formerly PharmGKB), the same knowledge base CPIC and PharmCAT draw on, so agreement between the Open Targets layer and PharmCAT is not independent corroboration. Annotations reflect curation at one release; a drug without PGx data is reported as no data, not as evidence of no effect.

Modifier panel. Small variants come from the distributed variant-only VCF; a position without a record may be reference or uncovered. Frequencies use the gnomAD joint population and group-maximum rather than an ancestry-matched subset. SpliceAI and AlphaMissense are predictions; SpliceAI does not score variants outside the MANE transcript bodies. Sensitivity for structural variants is limited for single-exon events, for balanced events with breakpoints in repeats, and for mosaic CNVs below roughly 30% of cells. A negative finding is bounded by these limits, and a modifier effect would need functional testing.

Mosaic aneuploidy. The sequenced tissue may not reflect the tissues driving the phenotype — the specimen type is not stated in the released metadata — and aneuploidy levels in MVA vary between tissues and over time; this is one tissue at one time point. Sensitivity depends on coverage depth and the number of informative heterozygous sites per arm; per-arm detection limits are reported, and a non-significant arm means below that limit, not absent. Arm-level testing is insensitive to random, non-clonal aneuploidy spread across chromosomes, the characteristic MVA pattern, so bulk WGS cannot serve as a sensitive pharmacodynamic readout of it. Single sample, no panel of normals; profile confidence intervals assume independent heterozygous sites. No karyotype or cytogenetic result was available for cross-checking.

Genome QC. Ancestry is assigned to 1000 Genomes superpopulations only. Runs of homozygosity use a default allele frequency rather than population-specific frequencies. The sex check infers chromosomal sex from sequencing data only.

Secondary findings. The analysis was scoped to the panels described and did not screen for ACMG secondary findings. Two pharmacogenes (RYR1, CACNA1S) are also on the ACMG secondary-findings list; they are reported only as pharmacogenes, with CPIC susceptibility classifications.

Readthrough axis. The functionality of the predicted readthrough products is a prediction (AlphaMissense), not a measurement, and AlphaMissense was trained to score substitutions in the reference protein rather than products of translational readthrough. The +4 base at this stop codon is the least permissive in the published hierarchy, so the context argues against the strategy even though the codon argues for it. The pairing the mechanism implies is bounded by what exists. No drug has been developed or approved for nonsense-mediated-decay inhibition, and a systematic profiling of FDA-approved drugs for NMD modulation found that most have unremarkable effects {{ cite('[@zhao2022]') }}. The approved agents with documented NMD-inhibitory activity are amlexanox; the cardiac glycosides, from a cell-based screen {{ cite('[@nickless2014]') }}; 5-azacytidine, at concentrations reported to correspond to levels reached in patients {{ cite('[@bhuvanagiri2014]') }}; and homoharringtonine, the one consistent hit in that FDA-wide profiling {{ cite('[@zhao2022]') }}. Pairing an NMD inhibitor with a readthrough agent has been tested in patient-derived cells for another nonsense disorder and raised protein from the mutant transcript {{ cite('[@amarschwartz2023]') }}, so the strategy is not hypothetical in general. It is this child that bounds it. Three of those four agents are cytotoxic or cardiotoxic: 5-azacytidine and homoharringtonine are myelosuppressive anticancer drugs, which would compound a regimen this report separately flags for therapy-related myeloid neoplasia risk in a chromosomal instability syndrome, and 5-azacytidine's NMD effect is MYC-dependent; the cardiac glycosides have a narrow therapeutic index, are renally cleared against a background of nephrocalcinosis, and would be added alongside an anthracycline. A mechanism-level concern sits underneath the drug-level ones: NMD is a surveillance pathway with tumour-suppressive and immune-surveillance roles, and each of those agents suppresses it globally, which in a cancer-predisposition syndrome is a risk none of them escapes. Whether an NMD-inhibitory exposure is reachable at a dose tolerable for this child is unresolved for every one of them, and none was carried as a candidate for that reason. That global-suppression objection is specific to small molecules: gene-specific antisense inhibition of NMD attenuates decay of a single transcript by blocking exon-junction-complex deposition downstream of the premature stop {{ cite('[@nomakuchi2016]') }}{{ cite('[@kim2022]') }}, and the mechanism section records it as the direction the mechanism points once the restriction to approved medicines is lifted. It is bounded by delivery rather than by selectivity, and it is not an approved medicine, so it is not a candidate here. Whether the small fraction of full-length protein that readthrough restores would cross the phenotypic threshold in this disease is unknown.

Proteostasis axis. There is no evidence in mosaic variegated aneuploidy or in any other aneuploidy syndrome, and no biomarker of proteotoxic stress is available for this child, so the axis rests on the documented cellular consequence of aneuploidy rather than on anything measured here.

Regimen re-examination. The antimitotic-resistance inference transfers from cancer cell lines with acquired checkpoint loss to a germline hypomorph, which is an inference and not a demonstration; the tumour's own checkpoint status is unknown because no tumour sequencing was released; and the published reduced-intensity case was available only as a citation, not as a full text, so no regimen detail is built on it.
